          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "2.1. Cliﬀord and Lie algebras from Hurwitz algebras",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            393.34,
            319.62,
            404.24
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMTI10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Unlike Lie and Cliﬀord algebras, there are only a ﬁnite number of Hurwitz algebras4. These",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            404.63,
            524.4,
            417.2
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10",
            "CMR8"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            7.97,
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "algebras are very generative. Given a Hurwitz algebra identiﬁed with a spinor space, one acts on",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            419.24,
            524.46,
            430.15
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "this spinor space with the algebra itself, from the left, right, or from both sides. Sometimes this",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            432.19,
            524.46,
            443.1
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "does not make a diﬀerence, and sometimes it does. Considering all the repeated nested actions",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            445.14,
            524.4,
            456.05
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "generate the adjoint algebras, which are speciﬁc Cliﬀord algebras. The bivectors of a Cliﬀord",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            458.09,
            524.44,
            469.0
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "algebra close under commutation, providing a representation of the Lie algebra that preserves",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            471.04,
            524.46,
            481.95
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "the metric of the associated pseudo-orthogonal space.",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            484.0,
            326.32,
            494.9
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The small set of Hurwitz algebras thus provide a means of selecting (or rather a mechanism for",
          "bbox": [
            85.04,
            496.95,
            524.42,
            507.86
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "generating) very speciﬁc Cliﬀord and Lie algebras from an otherwise inﬁnite sets. Particularly",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            509.9,
            524.46,
            520.81
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "interesting is the case of the complex octonions. Although this algebra is not associative, the",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            522.85,
            524.43,
            533.76
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "adjoint algebras Cℓ(7, 0) ∼= Cl(6) are.",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            532.65,
            249.49,
            547.21
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "3. Unbroken fermion symmetries from Hurwitz algebras",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            563.44,
            380.64,
            574.35
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMBX10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "3.1. Primitive idempotents and orthogonal subspaces",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            576.4,
            321.37,
            587.31
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMTI10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The extensive works of Dixon [12, 13, 14] focus on deriving the SM from tensor products of",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            589.35,
            524.47,
            600.26
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Hurwitz algebra.",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            602.3,
            152.37,
            613.21
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "The approach is to reduce spinor spaces of Hurwitz algebras (as well as",
          "bbox": [
            162.43,
            602.3,
            524.42,
            613.21
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "their associated action algebras) into sets of orthogonal subspaces. This is achieved by ﬁnding",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            615.25,
            524.44,
            626.16
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "projection operators within the algebras, corresponding to primitive idempotents obtained from",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            628.2,
            524.48,
            639.11
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "a resolution of the identity. In the case of C ⊗O the projectors can be used to split the algebra",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            638.77,
            524.43,
            652.06
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10",
            "CMSY10",
            "MSBM10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "into four orthogonal subspaces, each a SU(3) multiplet. This is because the automorphism group",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            654.11,
            524.43,
            665.01
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "that keeps e7 ﬁxed is SU(3). Each spinor in C ⊗O is resolved into 1 ⊕3 ⊕3 ⊕1 with respect",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            664.67,
            524.4,
            679.6
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMBX10",
            "CMMI10",
            "CMR10",
            "CMR8",
            "CMSY10",
            "MSBM10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            7.97,
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "to this chosen SU(3) automorphism group[12]. The same algebra admits a U(1) generator that",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            680.01,
            524.42,
            690.92
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMMI10",
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "produces the correct hypercharges.",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            692.96,
            236.9,
            703.87
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
          "fonts": [
            "CMR10"
          ],
          "sizes": [
            10.91
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "4 We use the term Hurwitz algebra here instead of normed division algebra because, the reduction of spinor",
          "bbox": [
            70.87,
            710.7,
            524.45,
            723.48
          ],
          "intersectsDrawing": false,
